Consolidate catalog `industry` column onto canonical 25-bucket taxonomy

The dropdown was showing 116 distinct industry strings — duplicates like
"Aerospace and Defense" / "Aerospace & Defense", "Airline" / "Airlines" /
"Airlines / Transportation", "CPG" / "Consumer Packaged Goods" /
"Consumer Goods" / "Consumer Products", "Pharma" / "Pharmaceutical" /
"Pharmaceuticals", "Tech" / "Technology", "Oil & Gas" / "Oil and Gas",
"Government" / "Public Sector", and dozens more.

Now every row is one of the 25 canonical buckets already defined in
`pipeline/enrichment/openai_industry_classifier.INDUSTRY_TAXONOMY`. The
mapping uses the existing `LEGACY_INDUSTRY_ALIASES` table (battle-tested
against the LLM classifier).

Also fixed a small bug in `_canonicalize()`: it didn't recognise canonical
names that weren't redundantly listed in their own variant tuples (e.g.
"Aerospace & Defense", "Hospitality & Travel", "Chemicals & Materials").
Now it checks the taxonomy directly first, then falls back to the alias map.

Three garbage values from earlier unconstrained LLM extraction passes
(e.g. "wide problem", "competitors, and competitive situation. Give the
following") were folded into "Other".

Production Postgres synced via `publish-cases` (467/467 rows updated).
